findings.claim:
§1.5.1 sets up the invariant theory on which the whole relative theory rests, and no item records it. Its contents are:
- the invariant-theoretic regular semisimple locus of a G-variety (closed orbit of maximal dimension);
- rational and stable orbits, the latter defined by a cocycle in Z^1(F, G_x^0);
- the bijection O_st(x) ≅ D(G_x^0/F) = ker(H^1(F, G_x^0) → H^1(F, G)), cited from [Kal11, Lemma 2.1.5];
- the abelianization 𝔠(G_x^0/F) = ker(H^1_ab(F, G_x^0) → H^1_ab(F, G)) [Bor98];
- injectivity D ↪ 𝔠 for abelian G_x^0, and bijectivity when F is nonarchimedean, which makes O_st(x) a 𝔠-torsor.
Items 7 (Lemma 2.3) and 19 (RO^κ) use 'rational orbits in the stable orbit of δ' for U(W_1) × U(W_2) acting on 𝔲(W)_1, which is not an adjoint action. ET.0 plans stable conjugacy only for groups acting on themselves: 'rational, geometric and stable semisimple conjugacy … cohomological parameterization of rational classes inside a stable class'.

The heart of the third reduction is the identity (5.1). It says that for |q(a)| = 1, O(a, (φ ∗ 1_0) ⊗ 1_{Λ_n}) = Orb^{U(V_{n−1})}(φ ∗ 1_0, y) and O(a, BC(φ) ⊗ 1_{L_n}) = ω(x) Orb^{GL_{n−1}(F),η}(BC(φ), x). This is what converts the (4.7)-hypothesis into vanishing of Φ_φ on |q(a)| ≥ 1. It has no item. Its ingredients have no items either:
- U(Λ_n) acts transitively on unit-norm vectors of Λ_n, and GL_n(O_F) on pairs (v, v*) ∈ L_n with v*(v) ∈ O_F^×; the stabilizers are U(V_{n−1}) and GL_{n−1}(F);
- the volume normalizations vol U(Λ_{n−1}) = vol GL_{n−1}(O_F) = 1;
- F(φ ⊗ 1_{L_n}) = φ ⊗ 1_{L_n} and F(φ′ ⊗ 1_{Λ_n}) = φ′ ⊗ 1_{Λ_n} for unramified ψ and self-dual lattices;
- SL_2(F) is generated by w and n(𝔭_F^{−1});
- local constancy of the functions a ↦ O(a, f) on 𝒜^rss(F).
Item 54's note summarizes the argument but asserts none of these. (This is the analogue of Lemma 1 in Beuzart-Plessis.)
